{
  "dom_id": "step-q127810",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{aligned}]$  is continuous.",
  "worked_solution": "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded points $t=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{t}$ is continuous for $t\\ge 0.$\n\n- The function $t+3$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for all $t\\ge 0$ except the point $t=1.$ So the interval of continuity is $[0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
